<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aeschines</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">389–314 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aeschines was an Athenian orator and statesman, the chief political opponent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a>. He favoured peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> and served on the embassies that negotiated the Peace of Philocrates in 346 BCE. After losing the famous lawsuit &quot;On the Crown&quot; against Demosthenes in 330 BCE, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and, according to tradition, taught rhetoric on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aeschines.jpg" alt="Aeschines" width="320" height="598" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aeschines_bust.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
